        "CAN_CIPO",
        "CAN_COPI"
      ],
      "observed": "On the CAN BUS schematic pages, both MCP2515 CAN controllers are wired with CAN_CIPO on pin 14 and CAN_COPI on pin 15 (U8: CAN_CIPO -> pin 14, CAN_COPI -> pin 15; U9: CAN_CIPO -> pin 14, CAN_COPI -> pin 15).",
      "expected": "For an MCP2515, pin 14 is SI (SPI input from the host) and pin 15 is SO (SPI output to the host). Using the design's own SPI naming convention, CAN_COPI (Controller Out, Peripheral In) should connect to pin 14/SI and CAN_CIPO (Controller In, Peripheral Out) should connect to pin 15/SO.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "CAN BUS page: U8 (MCP2515-xSO) left-side labels show CAN_CIPO at pin 14 and CAN_COPI at pin 15; U9 (MCP2515-xSO) similarly shows CAN_CIPO at pin 14 and CAN_COPI at pin 15."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "COPI \"Controller Out, Peripheral In\" CIPO \"Controller In, Peripheral Out\""
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The SPI MOSI/MISO lines are swapped on both CAN controllers, so the CM4 cannot communicate correctly with U8 or U9. CAN0 and CAN1 would be nonfunctional, breaking the motor/actuator CAN interfaces and any e-stop signaling routed through those CAN connectors.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "An anomaly is detected: both MCP2515 CAN controllers have their SPI data lines reversed, with CAN_CIPO connected to SI pin 14 and CAN_COPI connected to SO pin 15, which prevents correct SPI communication with the CAN controllers."
}

